WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:09.520
The world we live in today is a global world. We no longer conduct business solely within

2
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.440
one country. Our applications must make the transition from one country to another seamlessly.

3
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:21.280
The XPG standards are defining what services must be available to affect this transition.

4
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.240
This process is called internationalisation. This involves the display and printing of

5
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.600
characters, both common ones and ones unique to individual languages, the format of date

6
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:37.680
and monetary fields, and the collating sequence of the characters.

7
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:46.080
Now, to get a better grip on this, let's first off define some terms. A character set is the set of

8
00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:52.320
characters that you see on your keyboards or your printers that you can print. Internally,

9
00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:58.160
those characters are represented by unique hexadecimal values in memory. The relationship

10
00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:03.520
between the hexadecimal value and the character diagram is defined in a list called a code page.

11
00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:12.000
ZOS itself has no support for code pages. TSO-ISBF has support for code pages like ZOS-UNIX,

12
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.400
but differently. Each country and region might also have a unique code page called the country

13
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.600
extended code page, which relates the hexadecimal values from 00 to FF into a character set with

14
00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.840
the different characters used, including the needed letters in that language.

15
00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:36.560
Data coming from ZOS datasets or from a workstation could be in any of the code pages shown

16
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:43.200
here. The special characters shown here are the 14 variant characters that are often different

17
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.600
between the code pages. The individual country extended code page includes characters

18
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:56.800
letters in their alphabets that also require unique considerations. This diagram illustrates

19
00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.240
some of the problems you meet in an international environment. Let's look at the various

20
00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.440
translation issues and some solutions. First, let's look at a relatively simple situation

21
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:15.200
in a USA environment. Here are some possibilities to consider. Customize your keyboard using the

22
00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.000
features of the emulator to create the correct hexadecimal coding for the characters.

23
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:26.400
If you are a TSO user, specify the omvsconvert parameter when you enter the shell.

24
00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.560
If you are using the zos-unix shell through the TCPIPR login or telnet support,

25
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:38.640
use the chcp shell command to convert from ASCII to the zos-unix code page.

26
00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:45.520
And when using oput, oget and ocopy, use the convert parameters on these statements.

27
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:53.120
But what about shell scripts and translation with international character sets?

28
00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:59.680
Since many shell scripts use special characters, they must run the code page they were created

29
00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:06.560
with. This means that users in other countries have to have their own translated copy of the

30
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:10.560
script or they'll have to change to the code page that goes with the script.

31
00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.200
The icopy command can be used to translate the scripts from one code page to another.

32
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:23.520
Here you see that hash sign is represented as something else when you change the code page.

33
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.320
This character has a special meaning in a shell script. If this character is changed to something

34
00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:34.560
else, well, the shell script will not work as it should. This visual illustrates the

35
00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.920
problem from another angle. Here is a script with 3270 emulator program in IBM

36
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:49.920
1047 USA code page and IBM 277 Danish code page. These are all things to watch out for

37
00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.200
when using international character sets. Let's wrap this up with a comparison of

38
00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.120
different functions in TSO and the Unix shell. Command processing in the shell is similar to

39
00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.320
command processing for C lists. In combination with the shell utilities, the shell provides

40
00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:12.400
a programming environment as rich as the REX environment in COS. Hierarchical files can be

41
00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:19.200
edited from COS using ISPF menus. The ISPF menus can be invoked by the TSO commands OEdit

42
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:27.280
and OBrowse. In the shell, the editor can be used to edit HFS files. In COS, SDSF is used to

43
00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:34.160
monitor and control batch jobs. The TSOE commands status, cancel and output can also be used.

44
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:38.800
In the shell, there are multiple commands to control jobs including PS,

45
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:45.920
jobs, kill, FG, BG and wait. Background jobs are submitted by the TSOE commands submit

46
00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:53.120
in a ZOS environment. In the shell, a command ended with an ampersand, runs in the background.

47
00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:59.040
You can also use the shell submit command. Now in the next module,

48
00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:02.480
we'll move on to address functions in COS Unix.
